diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce an outage in Location C. All four hosts (PC11–PC14) can reach their local default gateway and external networks. Evidence:
- PC11, PC12, PC13, and PC14 each successfully ping 192.168.200.1 (router4 eth0) with 0% loss and ~0.6–1.6 ms RTT, proving the “own gateway” is reachable.
- From Location C to another site: PC11/PC12/PC13 can ping 172.16.10.4 (PC4) with 0% loss, showing inter-site routing works.
- Upstream from the gateway: router4 has eth0=192.168.200.1/24 up, plus two uplinks up (eth4=192.168.15.2/24 and eth5=172.25.1.1/24). Its routing table contains RIP-learned routes and a default via 172.25.1.2. From PC11 I can ping both neighbors 192.168.15.1 and 172.25.1.2 successfully, confirming upstream adjacency and forwarding.

Mechanism: Because host-to-gateway pings and routed traffic to other sites both succeed, the L2 segment (switch5/switch6 to router4), router4’s L3 gateway, and the upstream routed core are all functioning.

Localization: No failing interface or process found on switch5/switch6 or router4; routing and links are healthy.

Root cause: No network fault present at this time; the reported outage does not reproduce.

Corrective fix: None required on the network. If users still report issues, check local host settings (DNS resolution, personal firewall), and verify they are pinging IPs (e.g., 192.168.200.1) rather than names. Also collect timestamps to see if there was a transient event to correlate with logs. === END ===